---
scope: src/app/learn/spec-driven-vs-intent-driven-development
alignment: unreviewed
description: The /learn page answering spec-driven development vs intent-driven development, with a direct answer, a comparison table and a fair account of the criticisms of spec-driven.
intent: >
  This page answers people searching 'spec-driven development vs intent-driven
  development'. It opens with one paragraph on how the two differ, then sets
  them side by side in a table on concrete dimensions, using 'spec-driven' the
  way searchers do. It treats spec-driven development fairly, stating its common
  criticisms (the waterfall feel, too much markdown to review) and saying
  plainly where intent-driven engineering answers them and where it does not.
  Every claim about another tool traces to the research. Readers leave able to
  choose, with links on to the definition page, the spec-kit alternatives page
  and the methodology docs.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - The page opens with one paragraph that says how spec-driven and intent-driven development differ, before any section or table.
    - A comparison table sets the two approaches side by side on concrete dimensions, describing spec-driven development in terms its own practitioners would recognise.
    - The common criticisms of spec-driven development, that it feels like waterfall and produces more markdown than anyone reviews, are stated fairly, each with where intent-driven engineering answers it and where it does not.
    - Every claim about spec-driven development, its critics or a named tool is real and traceable to the research.
    - The page links on to the intent-driven engineering definition page, the spec-kit alternatives page and the methodology docs.
  undesired:
    - A strawman of spec-driven development, such as a comparison that lists only its weaknesses or describes it in a way its practitioners would not recognise.
    - A page that claims intent-driven engineering answers every criticism of spec-driven development, hiding the costs the two share.
    - A table built on vague or marketing dimensions, or one where every row favours intent-driven.
    - A claim about a named tool or about the critics that the research does not support.
    - An opening that defines intent-driven engineering at length before saying how the two differ, work that belongs to the definition page.
    - A tool-by-tool comparison of spec-driven products, which belongs to the spec-kit alternatives page, in place of a comparison of the two approaches.
---
